					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-435303#post-3556811</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-435303/knee-deep-in-the-keter#post-3556811</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Mon, 24 Jul 2017 16:55:26 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>sirpudding</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>348648</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>&quot;Able&quot; is old-school US radio alphanumeric for the letter &quot;A&quot; (current NATO is &quot;Alfa&quot;). So &quot;Subject Able&quot; is &quot;Subject A&quot;.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-435303#post-3553237</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-435303/knee-deep-in-the-keter#post-3553237</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Mon, 24 Jul 2017 08:32:09 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Mai Selph</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1790004</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>Okay, then SCP-076-2 is &quot;Able&quot;, and the Biblical character is &quot;הֶבֶל&quot;.</p> <p>What I mean is, 076-2 is known as &quot;Subject Able&quot;, so &quot;Able&quot; is like his codename or something. The guy who is famous because he was killed may be &quot;Able&quot;, &quot;Abel&quot;, or whatever, but 076-2 is just &quot;Able&quot;.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-435303#post-3547859</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-435303/knee-deep-in-the-keter#post-3547859</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Mon, 24 Jul 2017 06:04:04 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>sirpudding</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>348648</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>הֶבֶל being Hebrew, lacks vowels. Both spellings appear to be valid English transliterations, AFAICT. &quot;Abel&quot; is the one that Bibles have mostly standardized on.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
